     "type": "application",
     "title": "Tonight, and Then the Exam",
     "content": "That is the whole program. Twenty lessons, front to back, one last time. Here is what has to survive the night, and how to sit what comes next.\n\n**Know this cold — the four from this lesson.**\n• **Fraud requires intent to deceive; abuse does not.** Abuse is practice inconsistent with sound fiscal, business, or medical practice that produces unnecessary cost.\n• **Stark is strict liability; the Anti-Kickback Statute requires knowing and willful intent.** Stark exceptions are mandatory; AKS safe harbors are voluntary.\n• **The seven elements** of an effective compliance program: written standards; compliance officer and committee; training and education; lines of communication including an anonymous hotline; monitoring and auditing; well-publicized disciplinary standards; prompt response and corrective action.\n• **The 60-day overpayment rule** — report and return an identified overpayment within 60 days of identification, or by the cost report due date if later. Retain it and you have created an FCA obligation.\n\n**Where students lose points.**\n• **Saying Stark requires intent.** It never has. If an answer choice hangs a Stark outcome on what someone meant, it is wrong.\n• **Treating a compliance hotline as optional.** A mechanism for anonymous reporting is element four. A program without one is defective on its face, no matter how good the policies are.\n• **Confusing a RAC with a MAC.** The MAC pays your claims and hears your first-level appeal. The RAC takes payments back after the fact and is paid a percentage of what it recovers.\n\n**Tonight.** Do not reread the book. Reread your **Know this cold** lists and the **confusion pairs** — fraud/abuse, Stark/AKS, RAC/MAC, incidence/prevalence, sensitivity/specificity, Privacy Rule/Security Rule, ICD-10-CM/ICD-10-PCS, MS-DRG/APC, prospective/retrospective. Say each pair out loud with its distinguishing feature. Then sleep. Retrieval degrades far faster from fatigue than from one missing fact.\n\n**The exam ahead.** Lessons 22 through 27 are six parts of twenty questions each — **120 questions**, comprehensive, at genuine credentialing difficulty. Sit them in order, in one session if you can, roughly 35 to 40 minutes per part.\n\n**How to sit it.**\n• **Read the last sentence of the stem first**, then read the whole stem. It tells you what is actually being asked, which is often narrower than the paragraph suggests.\n• **Answer the question asked**, not the topic. A stem about a coding scenario may be testing a compliance rule.\n• **Eliminate two, then decide between two.** Most items collapse quickly to a genuine pair.\n• **Watch the qualifiers.** Absolutes — always, never, only, must in every case — are usually wrong. Hedged language — generally, unless an exception applies, in most circumstances — is usually right, because that is how the regulations are actually written.\n• **Flag and move.** Do not spend four minutes on one item; you will find the answer in a later stem more often than you expect.\n• **Do not change an answer** unless you can name the specific reason the first choice was wrong.\n• **Score each part separately.** Lesson 28 asks for your per-part scores, not just a total, because the pattern across the six parts is what tells you which two weeks of review you actually need.\n\nYou have done the work. Go take it.",
