     "type": "application",
     "title": "Score Part 6 — Then Take All Six Parts to Lesson 28",
     "content": "Count your correct answers out of 20. One point each, no partial credit, no bonus for being close.\n\n**What your Part 6 score means**\n• **18-20 — Command.** You can classify a situation and name the rule that governs it. This is what a compliance analyst, a quality abstractor or an HIM supervisor is hired to do.\n• **15-17 — Solid.** You know the frameworks. The misses are almost always the adjacent-concept traps — RCA against FMEA, at-risk against reckless, Stark against Anti-Kickback. Re-read those three pairs and the score moves.\n• **12-14 — Passing but thin.** You recognize the vocabulary and lose the application. Rework the missed scenarios out loud, saying which axis decided each one: intent, timing, or level.\n• **Below 12 — Genuine gap.** This is the domain that ends careers rather than lowering grades. Re-read Lessons 20 and 21 end to end before you do anything else.\n\n**Map every miss to a lesson, by question number**\n• **Q1, Q2** — six IOM aims, Donabedian structure/process/outcome → **Lesson 20, Quality, Performance Improvement & Patient Safety**\n• **Q4, Q6, Q8** — choosing a PI methodology, choosing a PI tool, common vs special cause variation → **Lesson 20, Quality, Performance Improvement & Patient Safety**\n• **Q10, Q12** — RCA vs FMEA, sentinel vs adverse vs near miss → **Lesson 20, Quality, Performance Improvement & Patient Safety**\n• **Q14, Q16, Q18** — just culture, incident reporting, utilization review timing → **Lesson 20, Quality, Performance Improvement & Patient Safety**\n• **Q3, Q5, Q7** — fraud vs abuse, Stark vs Anti-Kickback, False Claims Act and qui tam → **Lesson 21, Compliance, Fraud & Abuse, Auditing & HIM Department Management**\n• **Q9, Q11, Q15** — the seven compliance elements, the 60-day overpayment rule, LEIE exclusion screening → **Lesson 21, Compliance, Fraud & Abuse, Auditing & HIM Department Management**\n• **Q13, Q17, Q19, Q20** — cloning and coding compliance, identifying the auditor, audit accuracy math, staffing math → **Lesson 21, Compliance, Fraud & Abuse, Auditing & HIM Department Management**\n\n**Two cross-references worth following.** If Q6 or Q19 exposed weak ground on denials and the claim itself, revisit **Lesson 17, The Revenue Cycle, CDI & Denials Management**. If any compliance question left you unsure where professional obligation ends and employer instruction begins, revisit **Lesson 5, The HIM Profession: Roles, Credentials, Ethics & Accreditation**.\n\n**Now finish the exam.** You have six part scores. Write them in a column — Part 1 through Part 6 — and go to **Lesson 28, Your Score, Your Gaps & Your Next Step**. That is where you add them into a single score out of 120, see which of the six parts is dragging the total, and decide what to do with the answer. Do not stop here with six loose numbers on a page. Total them.",
